<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Liliʻuokalani is seen the morning after her emergency evaluation, with two members of her ʻohana whom she asked to come, and is seen the same day by Dr. Abernethy-Sato of vascular neurology. The episode two days ago, about 20 minutes of right hand weakness and difficulty finding words while she was playing the piano, has not recurred. In the emergency department yesterday the CT showed no bleeding, the CT angiogram showed no blocked vessel and a calcified plaque narrowing the left internal carotid artery by less than 50%, and she passed the bedside swallow screen; she was given aspirin 325 mg and clopidogrel 300 mg about 18 hours after the symptoms began and went home. This morning&apos;s MRI shows a small new infarct in the left precentral cortex, so the episode was a minor stroke, not a transient ischemic attack. The echocardiogram shows normal pumping, a mildly enlarged left atrium and no clot. She has had no palpitations. She asks plainly what has happened, and wants her ʻohana to understand it too. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Emergency evaluation</td><td>CT: no hemorrhage. CT angiography: no occlusion; left internal carotid plaque under 50%. Swallow screen passed</td></tr><tr><td>MRI</td><td>8 mm acute infarct, left precentral cortex</td></tr><tr><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>Ejection fraction 60%; left atrium mildly enlarged; no thrombus</td></tr><tr><td>Antiplatelets</td><td>Aspirin and clopidogrel loaded about 18 hours after onset</td></tr><tr><td>Cognition</td><td>MoCA 26</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

