    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Minor ischemic stroke, left precentral cortex (I63.412)</content>. Diffusion MRI shows that the 20-minute episode was a minor stroke. Its cortical, embolic pattern raises the question of atrial fibrillation; the carotid plaque narrows the artery by less than 50% and is a less likely source. Dr. Abernethy-Sato agrees with the plan. [S]<list><item>Aspirin 81 mg and clopidogrel 75 mg daily for 21 days in all, then clopidogrel alone.</item><item>30-day ambulatory rhythm monitor applied today.</item><item>Atorvastatin 40 mg daily; lipid panel in four to twelve weeks.</item><item>BE FAST taught to her and her ʻohana.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypertension (I10)</content>. 148/86 mmHg the day after the event; treatment is best begun a few days after a stroke. [S]<list><item>Home readings twice daily for a week; if the average is 130/80 or higher, start losartan 25 mg daily, with potassium and creatinine two weeks later.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Blood pressure in the clinic and at home (target below 130/80 mmHg) and weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>148/86 mmHg; 58.0 kg; home monitor provided</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antithrombotic therapy and adherence</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Day 2 of 21 days of aspirin and clopidogrel; no bleeding</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Neurological status (NIH Stroke Scale at the event; modified Rankin Scale at 90 days) and new symptoms</td><td>At the event and with any change; modified Rankin Scale at 90 days</td><td>Completed today</td><td>NIH Stroke Scale 0 in the emergency department and today</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Brain MRI with diffusion</td><td>At the event</td><td>Completed today</td><td>8 mm acute infarct in the left precentral cortex</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Carotid and intracranial vessel imaging</td><td>At the event; follow-up only for treated or moderate or greater stenosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>CT angiography in the emergency department: left internal carotid plaque under 50%; no intracranial stenosis</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Echocardiogram</td><td>At the event</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ejection fraction 60%; left atrial volume index 37 mL/m2; no thrombus</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>ECG and prolonged rhythm monitoring for atrial fibrillation</td><td>ECG at baseline; at least 30 days of monitoring within six months of the event</td><td>Ordered</td><td>ECG at intake: sinus rhythm. 30-day patch monitor applied today</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lipid panel (LDL goal below 70 mg/dL)</td><td>Four to twelve weeks after starting a statin, then every three to twelve months</td><td>Ordered</td><td>LDL 132 mg/dL at intake; atorvastatin 40 mg started; panel due in four to twelve weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Diabetes screen (hemoglobin A1c)</td><td>At the event, then periodically</td><td>Up to date</td><td>5.5% at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Cognitive screen (MoCA)</td><td>After the event, again within the first year, then periodically</td><td>Completed today</td><td>MoCA 26</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-2, PHQ-9)</td><td>At the first post-stroke visit, then at least yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 1; PHQ-9 planned at the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Swallow screen</td><td>Before any food or drink after a stroke</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Bedside swallow screen passed in the emergency department; eating normally</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Falls risk</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No falls; steady gait</td></tr><tr><td>Self-